      generic: "renin angiotensin aldosterone system raas",
      aliases: ["RAAS", "RAS pathway", "renin angiotensin system", "renin angiotensin aldosterone pathway", "how RAAS works", "why RAAS raises blood pressure"],
      class: "Hormonal pressure-volume and renal-perfusion control pathway",
      classExampleNames: ["Lisinopril", "Losartan", "Sacubitril/valsartan", "Aliskiren"],
      classExampleKeys: ["lisinopril", "losartan", "sacubitril valsartan", "aliskiren"],
      classPathway: ["Cardiovascular and renal physiology", "Renin to angiotensin I to angiotensin II", "AT1 vasoconstriction and aldosterone signaling"],
      usedToTreat: "This is a physiology and medication-pathway card. It explains why ACE inhibitors, ARBs, ARNIs, and direct renin inhibitors lower pressure or albuminuria, and why those same drugs can cause hypotension, hyperkalemia, creatinine change, acute kidney injury in vulnerable patients, and fetal toxicity.",
      description: "The renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system is the body's pressure-and-volume rescue circuit. Juxtaglomerular cells release renin when renal perfusion pressure falls, sodium chloride delivery to the macula densa falls, or beta-1 sympathetic signaling rises. Renin cleaves liver-derived angiotensinogen to angiotensin I; angiotensin-converting enzyme, especially on vascular endothelium, converts it to angiotensin II. Angiotensin II activates AT1 receptors, constricting arterioles, preferentially supporting efferent glomerular tone, stimulating aldosterone and vasopressin, increasing proximal sodium reabsorption, and reinforcing thirst and sympathetic activity. That response helps preserve pressure and filtration during true volume loss. Chronic overactivity, however, sustains hypertension, albuminuria, ventricular remodeling, fibrosis, and congestion. RAAS drugs are useful because they interrupt that maladaptive signaling, but a patient who currently depends on it for perfusion can deteriorate when it is blocked.",
      mechanism: "The pathway begins when renin's aspartyl-protease activity cleaves angiotensinogen. ACE removes two amino acids from angiotensin I to create angiotensin II and also degrades bradykinin. AT1-receptor activation couples through Gq proteins, phospholipase C, inositol trisphosphate, and intracellular calcium to contract vascular smooth muscle. In the adrenal zona glomerulosa it increases aldosterone, which raises epithelial sodium-channel and sodium-potassium-ATPase activity in the distal nephron; sodium retention expands extracellular volume, while potassium and hydrogen secretion rise. Angiotensin II also constricts the efferent arteriole, helping maintain glomerular capillary pressure when renal inflow is low. ACE inhibitors reduce angiotensin II and preserve bradykinin. ARBs leave angiotensin II concentration intact or increased but block AT1 signaling. Aliskiren blocks renin upstream. Sacubitril preserves natriuretic peptides while valsartan blocks AT1. These different targets explain both shared risks and class-specific effects.",
