      aliases: ["ACE inhibitor", "ACEI", "ACEIs", "angiotensin converting enzyme inhibitors", "pril drugs", "what do ACE inhibitors do", "examples of ACE inhibitors"],
      class: "Angiotensin-converting-enzyme inhibitors with bradykinin preservation",
      classExampleNames: ["Lisinopril", "Enalapril", "Enalaprilat", "Captopril", "Benazepril", "Ramipril", "Fosinopril", "Perindopril"],
      classExampleKeys: ["lisinopril", "enalapril", "enalaprilat", "captopril", "benazepril", "ramipril", "fosinopril", "perindopril"],
      classPathway: ["RAAS blocker", "ACE or kininase II inhibition", "Less angiotensin II and aldosterone plus more bradykinin"],
      usedToTreat: "Hypertension, selected chronic kidney disease with albuminuria, heart failure with reduced ejection fraction when ARNI is not feasible, post-myocardial-infarction remodeling or survival indications for selected agents, and agent-specific cardiovascular-risk indications. The exact indication belongs to the individual drug, not every -pril.",
      description: "ACE inhibitors block the enzyme that converts angiotensin I to angiotensin II and degrades bradykinin. Less angiotensin II means less AT1-mediated vasoconstriction, aldosterone release, sodium retention, sympathetic reinforcement, efferent-arteriole constriction, and maladaptive cardiac or renal remodeling. More bradykinin promotes vasodilation but also explains the signature dry cough and part of the angioedema risk. The class can lower systemic and intraglomerular pressure, reduce albuminuria, and improve outcomes in selected heart-failure or post-MI populations. Those benefits do not make every creatinine rise harmless: marked decline can reveal volume depletion, bilateral renal-artery stenosis, NSAID injury, sepsis, or pressure that has fallen below autoregulatory reserve. Agent differences matter: captopril is short acting and food sensitive, enalapril is a prodrug with an IV active form, lisinopril is active and renally excreted unchanged, and fosinopril has compensatory hepatic and renal elimination.",
      mechanism: "ACE is also called kininase II. Inhibiting it lowers formation of angiotensin II and slows bradykinin degradation. Arteriolar relaxation lowers afterload; reduced aldosterone lowers distal sodium retention and potassium excretion; reduced efferent tone lowers intraglomerular pressure and albumin leakage. In the myocardium and vasculature, less AT1 signaling reduces hypertrophy, fibrosis, oxidative stress, and adverse remodeling. Bradykinin stimulates endothelial nitric oxide and prostacyclin, adding vasodilation. It also increases vascular permeability and sensory-nerve signaling, which helps explain cough and nonhistaminergic angioedema. Because the glomerulus may rely on angiotensin-II-mediated efferent constriction when inflow is poor, ACE inhibition can abruptly reduce filtration in bilateral renal-artery stenosis, severe dehydration, decompensated heart failure, or NSAID-associated afferent constriction. The same causal chain explains why the nurse monitors pressure, creatinine, potassium, and volume together.",
