      classExampleNames: ["Lisinopril", "Losartan", "Sacubitril/valsartan", "Aliskiren"],
      classExampleKeys: ["lisinopril", "losartan", "sacubitril valsartan", "aliskiren"],
      classPathway: ["RAAS comparison", "Enzyme inhibition versus receptor blockade", "Natriuretic-peptide preservation with ARNI"],
      usedToTreat: "Use this comparison to choose the correct mechanism and safety frame when a learner encounters hypertension, albuminuric CKD, HFrEF, post-MI care, ACE-inhibitor cough, angioedema history, or a proposed switch to sacubitril/valsartan.",
      description: "ACE inhibitors reduce angiotensin-II formation and bradykinin breakdown. ARBs block the AT1 receptor while leaving ACE and bradykinin metabolism largely intact. ARNIs pair an ARB, valsartan, with neprilysin inhibition, which preserves natriuretic peptides but also raises bradykinin-related concern. All three lower AT1 signaling and therefore share hypotension, hyperkalemia, efferent-arteriole GFR effects, and fetal toxicity. ACE inhibitors stand out for dry cough and angioedema because ACE normally clears bradykinin. ARBs have much less cough and are often selected for cough intolerance, but rare angioedema still occurs. Sacubitril/valsartan is not simply a stronger ARB: sacubitril preserves vasoactive peptides while valsartan prevents neprilysin-related angiotensin-II accumulation from driving AT1. That dual mechanism improves HFrEF outcomes in appropriate patients but requires a 36-hour ACE-inhibitor washout and avoidance in a history of angioedema.",
      mechanism: "ACE inhibition lowers angiotensin II upstream and increases bradykinin. AT1 blockade prevents angiotensin II from activating vasoconstriction and aldosterone even when angiotensin II concentration rises. Neprilysin inhibition raises natriuretic peptides, bradykinin, adrenomedullin, and other vasoactive peptides; natriuretic-peptide receptor signaling increases cyclic GMP, promoting natriuresis, vasodilation, and antifibrotic effects. Neprilysin also degrades angiotensin II, so blocking neprilysin alone could raise a harmful counter-signal; valsartan is included to block AT1. Combining sacubitril with an ACE inhibitor would impair bradykinin breakdown through two pathways, explaining the angioedema danger and washout. Aliskiren is a fourth route: it blocks renin itself and lowers downstream angiotensin I, angiotensin II, and aldosterone, but it has not replaced outcome-proven ACE inhibitor, ARB, or ARNI roles.",
