      nursingEssentials: ["For cough, assess timing, lung disease, reflux, infection, heart failure, and whether the symptom resolves after a supervised medication change. Do not label the cough as an IgE allergy.", "For swelling, assess voice, tongue, floor of mouth, swallowing, secretions, stridor, and respiratory effort immediately; prepare for early expert airway management rather than waiting for oxygen saturation to fall."],
      interactions: ["ACE inhibitor plus neprilysin inhibitor produces excessive overlap in peptide-degradation blockade, explaining the 36-hour separation rule.", "DPP-4 inhibitors, mTOR inhibitors, and other drugs have been associated with higher angioedema risk in some ACE-inhibitor contexts; review the whole medication list in a patient with swelling."],
      keyLabs: ["No lab rules out bradykinin angioedema. Airway anatomy and trajectory are the priority; tryptase or complement studies may help a specialist evaluate alternative or hereditary mechanisms later.", "For the underlying ACE inhibitor, continue monitoring pressure, creatinine/eGFR, and potassium."],
      nclexTraps: ["Dry cough and angioedema share a peptide pathway but do not share urgency. Cough prompts evaluation; tongue or throat swelling prompts emergency airway action.", "Angioedema can occur after years of apparently safe use. A long medication history does not exclude the ACE inhibitor."],
      sourceNote: "Current ACE-inhibitor and ARNI labeling plus AHA/ACC/HFSA guidance: https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=e3b14778-a57e-51d5-e053-2a95a90a9aad ; https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/lookup.cfm?setid=000dc81d-ab91-450c-8eae-8eb74e72296f ; https://professional.heart.org/en/science-news/2022-guideline-for-the-management-of-heart-failure/-/media/832EA0F4E73948848612F228F7FA2D35.pdf",
