      classPathway: ["Medication safety", "Excess AT1 and aldosterone suppression", "Hyperkalemia, hypotension, and renal-perfusion collapse"],
      usedToTreat: "This card explains why routine ACE-inhibitor plus ARB therapy is avoided, why aliskiren with ACE inhibitor or ARB is contraindicated in diabetes, and why Entresto must not be combined with a separate ACE inhibitor or ARB.",
      description: "Blocking more than one point in the RAAS can lower pressure or albuminuria further, but it also removes redundant physiologic supports for filtration and potassium excretion. ACE inhibitor plus ARB, ACE inhibitor or ARB plus aliskiren, and accidental ARB duplication with sacubitril/valsartan increase hypotension, hyperkalemia, and acute kidney injury without routine outcome benefit sufficient to justify the added harm. The risk is greatest when CKD, diabetes, older age, dehydration, heart failure, NSAID use, diuretics, potassium supplements, trimethoprim, or mineralocorticoid-receptor antagonists are present. Sacubitril/valsartan creates an additional rule: it already contains valsartan, so a separate ARB is duplicate therapy; an ACE inhibitor is contraindicated because combined ACE and neprilysin inhibition markedly increases angioedema risk. Medication reconciliation must recognize ingredients and pathways, not just brand names.",
      mechanism: "Each RAAS blocker reduces AT1 and aldosterone signaling by a different route. Stacking them produces greater efferent-arteriole dilation, lower glomerular capillary pressure, less aldosterone-mediated potassium secretion, and more systemic pressure reduction. When renal inflow falls, the kidney has less ability to compensate, so GFR can collapse. Hyperkalemia rises because distal nephron sodium reabsorption and potassium secretion lose aldosterone support while CKD limits potassium clearance. Aliskiren combination trials in diabetes showed increased renal impairment, hyperkalemia, and hypotension, supporting the contraindication. ACE inhibitor plus sacubitril also blocks two peptide-degradation pathways, allowing bradykinin-related mediators to accumulate and increasing angioedema risk beyond ordinary duplicate RAAS effects.",
