      usedToTreat: "Chronic heart failure according to current sacubitril/valsartan labeling and guideline-defined phenotype. In symptomatic HFrEF, ARNI therapy is recommended to reduce morbidity and mortality when appropriate; use in other ejection-fraction ranges is more selective.",
      description: "ARNI means angiotensin receptor-neprilysin inhibitor. The available therapy combines sacubitril, a prodrug whose active metabolite inhibits neprilysin, with valsartan, an ARB. Neprilysin normally degrades natriuretic peptides and several other vasoactive peptides. Inhibition increases natriuresis, vasodilation, cyclic-GMP signaling, and antifibrotic effects. Neprilysin also degrades angiotensin II, so inhibiting it alone could increase an opposing vasoconstrictor signal; valsartan blocks AT1 and prevents that counter-response. The combination is disease modifying rather than merely a diuretic: it changes maladaptive neurohormonal signaling and reduces heart-failure events in appropriate patients. Its practical dangers follow the same biology: hypotension from vasodilation, hyperkalemia and renal changes from AT1 blockade, fetal toxicity from RAAS disruption, and angioedema from vasoactive-peptide accumulation.",
      mechanism: "Sacubitril is converted by esterases to LBQ657, which inhibits neprilysin. Atrial and B-type natriuretic peptides activate particulate guanylyl cyclase receptors, raising cyclic GMP and promoting natriuresis, diuresis, vasodilation, reduced sympathetic tone, and less hypertrophy or fibrosis. Valsartan selectively blocks AT1-mediated vasoconstriction, aldosterone, sodium retention, and remodeling. BNP is a neprilysin substrate and may rise after treatment, while NT-proBNP is not directly degraded by neprilysin and may better track changing wall stress. Bradykinin and other peptides can also increase, explaining the contraindication with ACE inhibition, the 36-hour washout, and avoidance in patients with angioedema history.",
